00:00:30.531 --> 00:00:34.810
AMIT GANDHI: OK, so today's
topic is going to be biogas

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:35.590
digesters.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.690
So our objectives for the
class, or this session,

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:42.640
are going to be to learn
how biogas digesters

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.180
work, when and where
they're appropriate,

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.730
some important design
considerations when you're

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.290
building biogas digesters.

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:53.155
And then we're going to build
a small-scale one in lab.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:59.715
So you get to play
with some of the stuff.

00:00:59.715 --> 00:01:01.580
Oops, I slipped slides.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.880
So these are some pictures
of biogas digesters.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:08.110
They've been around for a
couple hundred years, or maybe

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:08.890
150 years.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:15.220
There are lots of different
forms for them, as you can see.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.270
They scale up.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.930
They can be anywhere from large
industrial ones, like that one,

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.950
or they can be smaller scale
ones for just a single farmer

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.130
or single family.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.040
And what is a biogas
digester is the question

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.570
that you may be asking.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:34.540
So a biogas digester
is something

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.980
that takes organic
matter, normally animal,

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:43.660
human, vegetable waste, and
turns it into gas for cooking

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.810
and also turns it into
fertilizer for crops.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:49.840
So it's basically
taking organic matter

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.940
and putting it
through a process,

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:55.360
and you get gas and
fertilizer from that.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.860
The process that breaks
down the organic matter

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.780
is anaerobic digestion.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:07.960
So you have bacteria, which
are found in the organic waste.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.539
And when you put them under
anaerobic conditions, which

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:12.910
means that they're
not exposed to oxygen,

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:18.760
they break down all the organic
waste and form methane gas

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:19.555
and carbon dioxide.

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:26.920
And so you basically have
a giant chamber, I guess.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:31.810
And in this chamber, you
have your waste here,

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.730
with your bacteria in it.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:35.295
And you let it sit for a while.

00:02:35.295 --> 00:02:36.670
It takes about
two weeks for them

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.130
to start working once
you get them going.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:45.370
And they break down the organic
matter in here and produce gas.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:49.060
And you normally have
an inlet and an outlet.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:52.270
And what happens is, you
put in more organic matter

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:55.600
through the inlet, and it
pushes out some of the slurry

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:56.900
or whatever's left over.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.990
And so you get
fertilizer from this end.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:02.140
And the entire time you're
producing gas, which

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:03.760
you can extract from there.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:13.240
So about biogas, the
composition of biogas

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.670
is mostly methane
and carbon dioxide.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:20.890
So you have about 60% methane,
about 40% carbon dioxide.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:24.610
And then there are trace amounts
of nitrogen, hydrogen, hydrogen

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:30.490
sulfide, and oxygen.
And the amount

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:34.120
of energy you can
extract from biogas

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:45.425
is 20 megajoules per cubic
meter, which is a fair bit.

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:45.925
Yes.

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:48.010
AUDIENCE: How does
that 20 megajoules

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:53.300
compare to, say, diesel or
traditional fossil fuel?

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.562
AMIT GANDHI: I'm not
exactly sure right now.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:00.800
Yeah, I'll get back
to you on that.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.953
I had a chart with how
it compares to, like, how

00:04:03.953 --> 00:04:04.870
it could light things.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:06.708
But I didn't put it
on here because--

00:04:06.708 --> 00:04:07.208
yeah.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.480
And so the parts of
the biogas digester

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.329
are kind of like
what I drew here

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.399
but better, in this picture.

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:22.180
You have a trench where you
put the biogas digester.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.230
You have the biogas
digester bag,

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.470
which is what contains
all of the manure

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:28.780
and the slurry and the gas.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:30.280
Then you have your
inlet and outlet.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:33.160
And you have a security valve.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.740
And you have a gas
reservoir as well.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:40.840
So we're going to
talk a little bit now

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:44.470
about why you would
have a biogas digester.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.810
What are the advantages and
what are the disadvantages?

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.380
So now that you've learned
a little bit about it,

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.530
we can brainstorm some ideas
about why you would use it

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:56.270
or where you would use it.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:56.770
Yeah.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:59.280
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
water and waste,

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.345
does that mean you add water to
it, or it's just whatever is--

00:05:03.345 --> 00:05:08.490
AMIT GANDHI: You add water
to it to make a slurry.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:11.890
So you mix manure and water,
and you get the slurry.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:13.675
And then you put
it into the system.

00:05:13.675 --> 00:05:15.550
You guys will be doing
that later today, too.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:19.530
So I went to a farm and got
some fresh cow manure for you

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:20.430
to work with.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.860
So yeah.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:26.020
Yeah.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.918
AUDIENCE: What are the
typical sizes of [INAUDIBLE]??

00:05:31.762 --> 00:05:34.390
AMIT GANDHI: If you
want a ballpark size,

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:38.770
I guess would be maybe
like 10 or 15 feet.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:41.140
Does that seem right?

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.170
15-feet-long, maybe
like this size diameter.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.750
Your trench would be kind
of like this corridor

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:48.640
between the two tables.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.680
That's about the
size that you're

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:57.350
looking at for a
family, a household one.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.110
But they can range from
small to large as well.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:05.740
So if you look at
this, there's actually

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:10.190
a lot of design that went into
the specific biogas digester.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:13.840
And so we can look at the
individual components of it

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.100
and figure out why, and try to
look through the design process

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.570
and thought process
that they used

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:20.737
to come up with this design.

00:06:20.737 --> 00:06:22.810
Because this is actually
a pretty clever design

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:23.800
for a biogas digester.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.080
So does anyone notice
anything about this diagram

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:33.460
that seems like a cool--
like a good idea for them

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:34.770
to have done?

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:37.270
AUDIENCE: Security valve?

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:40.840
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah, the
security valve is important.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:44.455
You don't want your
thing blowing up.

00:06:44.455 --> 00:06:46.760
AUDIENCE: Reservoir?

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:50.560
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah, reservoir is
also good because, with this--

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:52.910
because storing gas is
also somewhat of a problem.

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:57.310
And if you don't have a
place to store the gas

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.675
and access it pretty easily,
then you're not going to have--

00:07:00.675 --> 00:07:01.175
yeah.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:09.320
Can you guys think
about the fence?

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:11.570
I don't know if you
can see it in there,

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:15.823
but there's a fence around
this specific design.

00:07:15.823 --> 00:07:17.990
And the problem-- one of
the things with this design

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:22.280
is that the biogas
digester bag for this

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:26.360
is a big plastic
bag that's covered

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:27.960
in tarp that's sewn together.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.160
And so you have this
giant plastic bag

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:32.720
that's sitting outside.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:34.178
And one of the cool
things about it

00:07:34.178 --> 00:07:35.762
is it's really cheap
because you don't

00:07:35.762 --> 00:07:37.880
have to build an external
construction for it.

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:41.462
But it's also-- like,
if you have cattle,

00:07:41.462 --> 00:07:43.920
which a lot of times you will
have for something like this,

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.940
and your Cow steps on this
bag, it's pretty much ruined.

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:50.285
You're ruining your, like,
$300 biogas digester.

00:07:54.478 --> 00:07:56.020
So that's why they
put a fence there.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:01.180
Can you think of
other advantages

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:05.380
to having a bag, other than
just the fact that it's cheaper?

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:08.530
Yes.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:11.680
AUDIENCE: Is it somewhat
mobile, transportable?

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:13.995
AMIT GANDHI: It's not
really transportable but--

00:08:13.995 --> 00:08:16.274
AUDIENCE: Like, if you
had to move location

00:08:16.274 --> 00:08:18.914
and you bought a new house,
can you take it with you?

00:08:18.914 --> 00:08:20.610
AMIT GANDHI: Oh, you
can't really move--

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:24.750
well, I mean, it's
easier to transport

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:26.220
to the site you're
installing to.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.050
So you can make one
of these and, I guess,

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.730
transport them to the location.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:31.147
I haven't actually
seen that done.

00:08:31.147 --> 00:08:33.600
I've seen most of them
being installed onsite

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:34.620
from the raw materials.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:38.039
But it's definitely easier
to get a large plastic bag

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:40.350
than it is to get
all of the bricks you

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:44.670
would need to build a giant
biogas digester and seal it.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:45.900
Yeah.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:51.580
AUDIENCE: Since you want to be
able to control the seal on it,

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:55.500
but since you have a bag, the
volume can actually change.

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:58.200
Even though you say that
it doesn't create a vacuum,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.035
is it--

00:08:59.035 --> 00:08:59.785
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah.

00:08:59.785 --> 00:09:01.360
AUDIENCE: ----[INAUDIBLE]
the volume changing?

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:03.485
AMIT GANDHI: So that's
actually a pretty cool thing

00:09:03.485 --> 00:09:06.310
about this design
is your bag can

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:08.740
get bigger and smaller,
depending on how much gas

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:10.810
is being produced.

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:16.090
Because if you have a rigid
structure, kind of like--

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:18.010
OK.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:19.720
I'm just going to
redraw what I just had.

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:22.750
So you have a box, and it's
say it's made out of bricks.

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:24.920
You somehow manage to
seal it from the inside

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:28.310
so there's no air
loss or gas loss--

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:30.445
which isn't trivial, for one.

00:09:30.445 --> 00:09:31.570
But you managed to do that.

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:34.120
And you put biogas in here.

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:38.860
And it's filling up.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:43.315
And it's producing methane, or
I guess it's producing biogas.

00:09:49.900 --> 00:10:00.180
What happens as you produce more
and more gas in this design,

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:03.070
you start getting a pressure
buildup of gas in here.

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:08.535
And if you're outputting it
into, say, a stove here--

00:10:13.373 --> 00:10:14.790
if you are putting
it into a stove

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:17.100
there, depending on
what the pressure is

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.080
of the biogas in
this chamber, you're

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.720
going to get a fluctuation
in your stove temperature--

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:24.630
or your flame front, which is
kind of annoying and tricky

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:27.240
to work with.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:30.420
So if you have something
that expands, like a bag,

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:31.380
it's better.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:34.960
You'll have more of
a constant pressure.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:36.720
There are some
other designs that

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:47.783
also try to regulate the volume
of the chamber for the gas.

00:10:47.783 --> 00:10:49.200
There's one called
a floating dome

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:51.180
one, which is actually
what you'll be making.

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:56.250
And what it is, you
have a giant thing here.

00:10:56.250 --> 00:11:01.090
You have your container,
which is solid, in this case.

00:11:01.090 --> 00:11:05.490
You have your slurry here.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:08.040
And then you have
a dome like this

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:11.880
that has your pipe coming out.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:15.030
And what happens is this
can move up and down inside

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:16.320
of your chamber.

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:19.380
And so, as you produce
more methane gas--

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:24.600
or biogas, rather-- as
the pressure builds up,

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:28.470
it pushes up on the dome so
you're creating more of a space

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:32.490
and regulating pressure with
the size of the chamber, which

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:33.780
is a pretty cool design.

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:36.180
And the ones you guys
are going to be making

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.760
are kind of similar to this.

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:42.060
But some of the adaptation
that are going to be--

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:45.310
you're going to take the
bottom of a Coke bottle.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:48.490
It's kind of like that.

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:50.920
And then you're going
to fill it with water.

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:53.670
And you're going to
have one biogas chamber.

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:56.530
You're going to have
your chamber with slurry.

00:11:56.530 --> 00:11:59.070
And then you're going to
have a valve coming into here

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:02.530
and coming out of the water
and going up into that chamber.

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:08.340
So this will be able to move,
based on how much gas you

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:10.914
have in there.

00:12:10.914 --> 00:12:13.050
Can you guys think of
any other advantages

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:19.390
to having this sort of
design, other than being

00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:20.875
able to regulate
a constant amount?

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:30.030
So how would I know
how much biogas

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:32.155
I'm going to have in this
chamber with this design?

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:37.330
I would have to put basically
a pressure valve on here

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:44.080
and read pressure, which is
kind of annoying and expensive.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:46.900
And it's not it's not really
easy to get a pressure

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:49.510
valve, in a lot of places.

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:55.060
But with a design like
this, if you let it float,

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:57.550
you can measure just the
height of this chamber

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:00.500
because the higher the chamber,
the more volume of biogas

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:01.000
you have.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:02.230
So it's almost like
an indicator that

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:03.800
tells you how much
you have as well,

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:07.420
which is also pretty cool.

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:11.950
But I've talked a lot about
how biogas digesters work,

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:15.460
in the design sense.

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:18.640
But we haven't really
got into why they're used

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:22.370
or where they would
be applicable.

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:28.720
So let's think about what
are the advantages of biogas

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:31.850
to other alternative
cooking fuels?

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:35.185
We can start by naming
other sources of fuel.

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:42.350
Someone want to throw
out other sources of fuel

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:43.560
that people use?

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:44.435
So you have charcoal.

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:52.770
You have wood.

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:03.210
Anyone seen other
techniques that they use?

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:04.990
AUDIENCE: Natural gas.

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:06.055
AMIT GANDHI: Natural gas.

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:17.390
I've seen, in other
parts of the world,

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:20.855
people actually just
burn manure straight.

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:33.520
I guess you also
have electricity.

00:14:37.264 --> 00:14:40.210
AUDIENCE: Wait, biogas
comes from manure though.

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:40.960
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.425
AUDIENCE: Does biogas
mix with the fuel

00:14:43.425 --> 00:14:47.182
or if manure is the fuel,
how does manure fuel

00:14:47.182 --> 00:14:49.166
without any processing?

00:14:49.166 --> 00:14:51.820
AMIT GANDHI: In places, they
just take, like, cow dung

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:53.830
and just burn it
as a fuel source.

00:14:53.830 --> 00:14:54.790
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:56.832
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah, they
just take it and burn it.

00:14:56.832 --> 00:14:59.678
And then you cook
your food over it.

00:14:59.678 --> 00:15:01.720
AUDIENCE: Well, then, what
did the wood burning--

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:05.062
or how do they burn it with, or
how do they light it on fire?

00:15:05.062 --> 00:15:07.090
AUDIENCE: It gets dried out.

00:15:10.158 --> 00:15:12.200
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah, you
let it sit out in the sun.

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:12.840
It dries out.

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:14.240
It forms a briquette.

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:15.950
And you can just
burn this briquette.

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:19.130
And then it releases
energy when you burn it.

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:23.040
Yeah.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:24.952
AUDIENCE: It gives off
a lot of bad smoke.

00:15:24.952 --> 00:15:26.390
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah.

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:29.350
It gives off a lot of
bad smoke, bad smell too.

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:33.650
And the other thing
about burning manure

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:39.170
directly is that it's kind
of taking a product that's

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:41.300
very rich in
resources, like there's

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:43.400
a lot of organic
matter in there that

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:45.030
could be used as fertilizer.

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:48.050
And instead of using these
good properties of that,

00:15:48.050 --> 00:15:50.840
you're just combusting it.

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:53.870
So it's a waste of
resources, basically,

00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:55.190
to burn manure directly.

00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:02.000
What about wood and charcoal?

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:04.970
What are disadvantages of that?

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:15.650
AUDIENCE: Besides where you're
using the D-Lab method of using

00:16:15.650 --> 00:16:17.240
agricultural waste
to make charcoal,

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:19.370
it leads to deforestation
[INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:26.570
AMIT GANDHI: OK.

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:29.210
That's definitely a big concern.

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:33.710
If you look at Haiti, so
much of Haiti is deforested.

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:37.946
And in places where you
don't have access to--

00:16:37.946 --> 00:16:42.440
like, in a lot of parts of,
I guess, India and Nepal,

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:45.057
you'll have areas that are
heavily deforested already.

00:16:45.057 --> 00:16:47.390
And so they don't have access
to wood and charcoal even.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:48.980
And charcoal is expensive there.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:53.960
And in places like that, where
people are farmers or cattle

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:57.770
farmers, a technology like
biogas digesters makes sense.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:08.244
Any other disadvantages
to wood, maybe?

00:17:08.244 --> 00:17:09.119
Have you ever tried--

00:17:09.119 --> 00:17:09.899
AUDIENCE: Collection.

00:17:09.899 --> 00:17:10.589
AMIT GANDHI: What?

00:17:10.589 --> 00:17:12.089
AUDIENCE: Collection,
time involved.

00:17:18.317 --> 00:17:22.900
It's hard to keep
it good and dry.

00:17:36.955 --> 00:17:38.830
AMIT GANDHI: Just as a
sense, have any of you

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:41.260
been to developing world
places where you've

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:43.300
seen people cook on three--

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:47.290
stone, wood fires, or
anything like that?

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:52.722
What did you notice about
the cooking process?

00:17:52.722 --> 00:17:54.672
AUDIENCE: It's
pretty inefficient.

00:17:54.672 --> 00:17:56.130
AMIT GANDHI: Do
want to tell people

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:57.870
about how they cooked there?

00:17:57.870 --> 00:17:59.698
AUDIENCE: Well, [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:59.698 --> 00:18:01.069
It was my aunt's house.

00:18:01.069 --> 00:18:03.830
It was-- I mean, it's
just hard to come up--

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:08.059
it's just hard to get the fuel.

00:18:08.059 --> 00:18:10.007
So a lot of times
you just don't cook.

00:18:10.007 --> 00:18:15.850
And they end up just
not being able to cook.

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:16.830
AMIT GANDHI: OK.

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:19.350
When they did cook,
what was the process

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:22.950
that they used for
cooking with the wood?

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:25.005
Because it varies,
depending on where you are.

00:18:25.005 --> 00:18:27.630
There's a lot of different ways
of combusting wood and using it

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:27.990
in the stove.

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:29.240
What did that stove look like?

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:30.576
AUDIENCE: I was a lot younger.

00:18:30.576 --> 00:18:32.430
This was maybe six years ago.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:34.222
So I think it was just
like buying the wood

00:18:34.222 --> 00:18:36.006
and putting the
[INAUDIBLE] the wood.

00:18:36.006 --> 00:18:38.220
AMIT GANDHI: OK.

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:39.100
You've--

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:40.830
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I'm not
sure what you're getting at.

00:18:40.830 --> 00:18:41.670
AMIT GANDHI: Like--

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:42.464
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:42.464 --> 00:18:43.260
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:44.370
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah, OK.

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:45.000
Well--

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:49.076
AUDIENCE: It's just, like, hard
to regulate the temperature.

00:18:49.076 --> 00:18:52.932
You have to keep feeding wood
to it to keep it burning.

00:18:52.932 --> 00:18:53.930
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah.

00:18:53.930 --> 00:18:56.390
And one of the biggest
problems with it

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:58.580
is also just the
smoke that it releases

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:05.960
and the fumes from that,
which are both pretty bad.

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:08.270
Wood fires cause a
lot of pollution.

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:11.930
And most people cook with wood
fires inside their houses.

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:13.940
And indoor air pollution
is a really big problem

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:15.360
throughout the developing world.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:19.100
And so biogas has the advantage,
where it doesn't release

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:21.680
any of these toxic fumes.

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:22.180
Yeah.

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:25.450
AUDIENCE: Do people complain
about taste with the biogas?

00:19:25.450 --> 00:19:26.620
I'm not saying that, like--

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:30.070
but I know several Colombian
people cook a lot on wood

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:31.120
just for the taste.

00:19:31.120 --> 00:19:32.740
They say it tastes better.

00:19:32.740 --> 00:19:35.920
So is that a drawback?

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:39.134
That maybe it doesn't give
the same taste as wood,

00:19:39.134 --> 00:19:40.625
as an open fire.

00:19:40.625 --> 00:19:42.116
AUDIENCE: Or is it even fire?

00:19:42.116 --> 00:19:44.058
AUDIENCE: Yeah, right,
[INAUDIBLE] or it

00:19:44.058 --> 00:19:46.503
could be bad [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:46.503 --> 00:19:47.920
AMIT GANDHI: I
haven't seen-- have

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:50.270
you seen that under concern?

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:53.983
I think the way a lot of
people I've seen use--

00:19:53.983 --> 00:19:55.900
how are you talking about
them using the wood?

00:19:55.900 --> 00:19:58.330
Are you talking about
them barbecuing something

00:19:58.330 --> 00:19:58.900
or grilling?

00:19:58.900 --> 00:20:01.660
AUDIENCE: No, so what
I remember, it was just

00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:05.165
bringing the wood and
then those giant pots--

00:20:05.165 --> 00:20:06.040
what are they called?

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:11.380
And they cook, like, a soup
in there, or a chicken.

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:13.120
And even then, it's--

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:14.610
oh, it's wood-fire cooking.

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:20.303
It's currently, you know, how my
mom used to make it, basically.

00:20:25.383 --> 00:20:27.800
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah, I don't
have much experience with that.

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:29.600
So, sorry, I can't help.

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:30.100
Do you?

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:33.615
AUDIENCE: I think it
is a behavioral change.

00:20:33.615 --> 00:20:34.990
AUDIENCE: I think
a lot of it has

00:20:34.990 --> 00:20:37.613
to do with what techniques
do I bring with [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:37.613 --> 00:20:41.061
Because with biogas, you
have to have gas stoves.

00:20:41.061 --> 00:20:44.010
You have to have
that sort of thing.

00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:47.915
So I know that that's some
problem that they have.

00:20:47.915 --> 00:20:51.651
[INAUDIBLE] differently.

00:20:51.651 --> 00:20:53.960
AMIT GANDHI: OK.

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:55.025
What about natural gas?

00:20:57.938 --> 00:21:00.355
What are some of the problems
associated with natural gas?

00:21:04.676 --> 00:21:05.176
Yes.

00:21:05.176 --> 00:21:09.150
AUDIENCE: It's expensive
and it's nonsustainable.

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:12.840
Unless It's produced
literally in your area,

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:14.878
you have basically [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:20.290 --> 00:21:25.008
AUDIENCE: You also need
the lines to connect to you

00:21:25.008 --> 00:21:26.490
if you're going to have--

00:21:30.936 --> 00:21:33.900
AUDIENCE: So can it
be kind of dangerous?

00:21:36.367 --> 00:21:37.450
AMIT GANDHI: I don't know.

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:39.040
I don't think of it as
particularly dangerous.

00:21:39.040 --> 00:21:40.840
It just-- you need a
lot of infrastructure

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:42.790
to be able to provide
natural gas to people.

00:21:42.790 --> 00:21:48.100
And some places it's
available and not too bad.

00:21:48.100 --> 00:21:52.060
When I was working in a favela
in Brazil over this IAP,

00:21:52.060 --> 00:21:55.000
they had a truck come
in with natural gas--

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:56.230
or LPG.

00:21:56.230 --> 00:21:58.790
And they would bring it
every couple of days,

00:21:58.790 --> 00:22:01.240
and at the same time, and
pick up old containers

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:04.270
and give people new containers
for a certain price.

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:06.010
And that seemed
like it was great.

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:08.950
But you can't really have that
same sort of infrastructure

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:11.365
in a more rural
place, for example.

00:22:14.530 --> 00:22:16.310
Manure, we already talked about.

00:22:16.310 --> 00:22:23.290
And people don't really
cook with electricity

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:28.180
that much in the developing
world, that I've seen.

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:34.390
So yeah, biogas has
a lot of advantages

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:36.250
to these other cooking methods.

00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:40.870
But it also requires
certain things.

00:22:40.870 --> 00:22:43.660
You basically need a
lot of manure or waste

00:22:43.660 --> 00:22:46.090
to go into this biogas
digester every day.

00:22:46.090 --> 00:22:48.220
I think the minimum
recommendations

00:22:48.220 --> 00:22:52.630
are having maybe two cows
or something like that,

00:22:52.630 --> 00:22:53.890
or a few pigs.

00:22:53.890 --> 00:22:57.700
And if you don't meet
these conditions,

00:22:57.700 --> 00:23:01.090
you're not going to be able
to have a biogas digester that

00:23:01.090 --> 00:23:04.070
produces that much fuel
for you to cook off of.

00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:06.280
And the investment
for building one

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:12.880
isn't paid back over a
reasonable period of time.

00:23:12.880 --> 00:23:14.740
The other thing that's
cool about biogas

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:18.640
is that when you pass your--

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:20.530
I think if you're not
using human waste,

00:23:20.530 --> 00:23:22.000
if you're using
manure, and you're

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:24.670
using agricultural waste
or inorganic waste,

00:23:24.670 --> 00:23:26.830
if you pass it through
the biogas digester

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:28.810
at the end of it, you
get fertilizer, which

00:23:28.810 --> 00:23:32.140
is really good because you're
taking a waste product right

00:23:32.140 --> 00:23:36.070
now, which is animal
waste, and it smells bad,

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:39.910
and people try to dispose of
it in ways where they're not

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:42.740
utilizing any value from it.

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:46.630
And you're getting
value from this product,

00:23:46.630 --> 00:23:49.270
which is trash right now,
which is a really cool idea.

00:23:49.270 --> 00:23:54.850
Plus, when you pass the waste
through the biogas digester,

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:56.740
a lot of the harmful
bacteria get killed off

00:23:56.740 --> 00:23:58.400
in the process of producing.

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:00.400
Or like, when it goes
into anaerobic conditions,

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:03.100
a lot of the harmful
bacteria get killed off.

00:24:03.100 --> 00:24:06.880
So what you're left
with is good fertilizer

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:11.230
that you can use
to grow crops with.

00:24:11.230 --> 00:24:16.090
And, yeah, so it's really
just taking a waste product

00:24:16.090 --> 00:24:17.370
and getting value from there.

00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:23.800
What are some of
the problems you

00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:27.070
can see with biogas
digesters, based off

00:24:27.070 --> 00:24:30.670
of this cursory
introduction to them?

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:37.380
Yeah.

00:24:37.380 --> 00:24:40.280
AUDIENCE: You have
to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:42.030
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah.

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:44.730
Well, I guess they
just cost about--

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:49.950
I think the cheapest ones I've
seen are around $300 to build.

00:24:49.950 --> 00:24:52.200
But they're on the order
of hundreds of dollars.

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:55.770
And so that's pretty expensive
to shell out at once,

00:24:55.770 --> 00:24:59.220
to build one of these.

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:00.497
Another big thing, again--

00:25:00.497 --> 00:25:01.830
were you going to say something?

00:25:01.830 --> 00:25:04.230
AUDIENCE: Oh, no, I was
going to say maintaining it

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:09.030
and having to put
[INAUDIBLE] fertilizer.

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:10.280
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:13.320
The maintenance costs
are pretty big as well.

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:17.450
And the problem with this is
there's so many parts, as well,

00:25:17.450 --> 00:25:20.840
that are very critical to
the functionality of it,

00:25:20.840 --> 00:25:23.000
that if one of them
breaks, you have to fix it.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:24.480
Otherwise, your
entire thing stops.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:27.290
Like, if your bag gets a
hole in it, all of your gas

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:28.440
is going to leak out.

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:33.052
So you have to carefully
design it and make it so that--

00:25:33.052 --> 00:25:34.760
and you have to really
take care of this.

00:25:34.760 --> 00:25:40.860
Because if you don't, then it's
just not going to work anymore.

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:48.510
There are some common sources
of problems that biogas

00:25:48.510 --> 00:25:50.400
digesters have over time.

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:57.740
And those are just leaks,
temperature, and pH.

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:04.030
So last year, we did
this lab in D-Lab Energy,

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:06.580
as well, where we
made biogas digesters.

00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:10.510
And none of them produced
any methane gas or biogas.

00:26:10.510 --> 00:26:13.720
And it was because last
year, unlike this year,

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:15.320
D-Lab was very, very cold.

00:26:15.320 --> 00:26:17.918
It was-- like, you needed
to wear jackets around,

00:26:17.918 --> 00:26:19.960
or you just wouldn't be
able to walk around here.

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:24.790
And biogas digesters
need a warm temperature.

00:26:24.790 --> 00:26:30.500
I think around 50
degrees Celsius,

00:26:30.500 --> 00:26:32.440
though that seems high now.

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:34.583
No, that seems high.

00:26:34.583 --> 00:26:36.250
I'll get back to you
on the temperature.

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:39.110
But they have a pretty high
operational temperature.

00:26:39.110 --> 00:26:42.068
And if they're in that
range, then they work well.

00:26:42.068 --> 00:26:44.360
And if they're not in that
range, they don't work well.

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:47.050
So if you're in an area
that gets cold a lot

00:26:47.050 --> 00:26:51.580
or has huge temperature
shifts, then your bacteria

00:26:51.580 --> 00:26:54.220
that are producing methane
under anaerobic conditions

00:26:54.220 --> 00:26:57.910
are going to die out
because of the cold.

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:00.340
And you might have
to come up with some

00:27:00.340 --> 00:27:03.520
of heating device for
your biogas digester,

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:05.590
to keep it warm.

00:27:05.590 --> 00:27:08.823
The last thing is pH.

00:27:08.823 --> 00:27:10.240
For these bacteria
to survive, you

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:15.070
need to have a pH
that's around 7.

00:27:15.070 --> 00:27:16.900
So I think 6 to 8 is reasonable.

00:27:16.900 --> 00:27:20.560
And if your pH drops below
that or gets raised above that,

00:27:20.560 --> 00:27:23.650
then you also have problems
with biogas digesters working.

00:27:27.280 --> 00:27:32.180
So yeah, I guess
that's pretty much

00:27:32.180 --> 00:27:34.730
all I had prepared
for the biogas,

00:27:34.730 --> 00:27:37.610
just like for the
lecture-y part of this.

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:40.170
So do you have any questions?

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:40.670
Yes.

00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:42.530
AUDIENCE: Are the
bacteria introduced,

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:43.986
or are they [INAUDIBLE]?

00:27:43.986 --> 00:27:45.392
AMIT GANDHI: No,
they are found--

00:27:45.392 --> 00:27:47.600
I mean, the bacteria that
are in the biogas digesters

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:50.390
are the same bacteria that are
going to be in your stomach

00:27:50.390 --> 00:27:52.760
or cow stomachs.

00:27:52.760 --> 00:27:54.650
Yeah, so they're
just introduced.

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:57.710
Or not introduced, they're
just found in the manure.

00:27:57.710 --> 00:28:01.770
AUDIENCE: So for the
slurry that comes out,

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:03.750
can that be used
directly as fertilizer,

00:28:03.750 --> 00:28:05.130
or does it have to be composted?

00:28:05.130 --> 00:28:07.950
Because I know when
you use human waste,

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:10.932
you do have to compost it.

00:28:10.932 --> 00:28:14.220
AMIT GANDHI: So it needs
to be separated, at least.

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:17.640
Because there's sort of like the
liquid part and the hard part.

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:22.830
Or there's the liquid part
and the solid matter part.

00:28:22.830 --> 00:28:25.440
And a solid matter part
is generally composted,

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:27.750
and the liquid part
is used as fertilizer.

00:28:30.785 --> 00:28:31.680
Any other question?

00:28:31.680 --> 00:28:32.180
Yes?

00:28:32.180 --> 00:28:35.930
AUDIENCE: How do you
even collect cow manure?

00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:37.940
Do they actually put a
diaper thing on a cow,

00:28:37.940 --> 00:28:39.290
or what do they do?

00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:42.590
I don't know, actually,
how that works.

00:28:42.590 --> 00:28:46.375
AMIT GANDHI: I think they just
scoop it up afterwards, yeah.

00:28:46.375 --> 00:28:48.088
AUDIENCE: That seems
like less easy.

